    {
        "id": "warning-join",
        "type": "ui-markdown",
        "z": "yonder-console",
        "group": "group-net-join",
        "order": 1,
        "width": 6,
        "height": 6,
        "name": "what is about to happen",
        "content": "### Read this before you join a network\n\nThis board has **one Wi-Fi radio**. It can run its own access point, or it can join a network. It cannot do both.\n\nSo the moment you press Join:\n\n1. **The `yonder` access point disappears.** Your browser will lose this page. That is not a fault — it is the radio being retuned to the network you chose.\n2. Join that same network yourself, then open the console again at **`http://yonder.local:3000/dashboard`**. If that name does not work on your network or your device, look for the device in your router's list of connected clients and use its address instead. (See the note below.)\n3. **Come back within 5 minutes and press \"Yes, I can still reach it\".** If you do not, the device puts everything back the way it was and the access point returns by itself.\n\n**If the password was wrong, do nothing.** The device cannot join, so it gives up, restores the old configuration, and brings the `yonder` access point back on its own. Wait for the five minutes, rejoin `yonder`, and try again.\n\n---\n\n*About the name:* this device publishes `yonder.local` over mDNS. That works on macOS and iOS, on Windows 10 and later, and on many Android versions — but not on every network and not on every device, and it has not been verified on real hardware for this build. The router's client list always works.",
        "className": "yonder-warning",
        "x": 200,
        "y": 780,
        "wires": []
    },
